---
name: modulus-ui
description: Build pages, tables, forms, menus and themes for the {{ app_name }} app with the Modulus UI framework ({{ ui_engine }}). Use it for any change to the web host's UI.
---

# Modulus UI for {{ app_name }} ({{ ui_engine }})

The web host is `src/Web/{{ root_namespace }}.Web`. The shell (header, sidebar, theme settings and footer) comes from the
framework. You write the page content, the menu and the data calls.

{{ if ui_engine == "blazor" }}
## Pages and the shell

- A page is a component with `@page "/route"`. Its body sits inside `<AppShell>` (set in `Components/Layout/MainLayout.razor`).
- Put the title block at the top with `<PageHeading Pretitle="..." Title="..." Subtitle="...">` and buttons in `<Actions>`.
- Components come from `Synvro.Modulus.Blazor`. Ant Design components are named as in Ant Design Blazor (`Button`, `Input`,
  `FormItem`, `Switch`, `InputNumber`). The `App*` names are aliases with the same parameters (`AppButton`, `AppTable`).

## Tables: `ProTable<TItem>`

```razor
<ProTable TItem="Customer" @ref="_table" HeaderTitle="All customers" RowKey="c => c.Id"
          Request="LoadAsync" Selectable FiltersActive="!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_keyword)"
          OnClearFilters="ClearSearch">
    <ToolBar>...</ToolBar>
    <BatchActions Context="rows">...</BatchActions>
    <Columns>
        <PropertyColumn Property="c => c.Name" Sortable />
        <ActionColumn>...</ActionColumn>
    </Columns>
</ProTable>
```

- `Request` is `Func<ProTableRequest, Task<ProTableResponse<TItem>>>`. The request carries the page, the page size, the
  sort and the column searches. Apply them on the server, and return one page of rows.
- Other parameters: `PageSize`, `Selectable`, `RowSelectable`, `BatchActions`, `RowDetail`, `Filters` (a filter row),
  `UrlState` (keeps the page and sort in the URL), `MultiSort`, `GroupFields`, `HeaderGroups`, `Exportable`, `ColumnSettings`.
- Call `_table.ReloadAsync(resetPage: true)` after a change.

## Forms: `DrawerForm<TModel>` and `ModalForm<TModel>`

```razor
<DrawerForm TModel="CustomerInput" Title="@_title" @bind-Visible="_drawerOpen"
            Model="_input" OnFinish="SaveAsync" SubmitText="Save">
    <FormItem Label="Name"><Input @bind-Value="context.Name" /></FormItem>
</DrawerForm>
```

- `OnFinish` is `Func<TModel, Task<bool>>`. Return `true` when the save worked, and the drawer closes. Return `false`
  to keep it open.
- Validate with data annotations on the model. The form shows the messages next to the fields.

## Cards and numbers

- `StatisticCard` shows a value with a title, a tooltip and an optional footer.
- Charts: `LineChart`, `ColumnChart`, `BarChart`, `DonutChart`, `HeatmapChart`, `Sparkline`.

## Menu

```csharp
public sealed class AppMenuContributor : IMenuContributor
{
    public Task ConfigureMenuAsync(MenuConfigurationContext context)
    {
        context.Add(new MenuItemDefinition("customers", "Customers", "customers", "team") { Order = 10 });
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Register it with `builder.Services.AddTransient<IMenuContributor, AppMenuContributor>();`.
`Href` has no leading slash. Add `RequiredPolicy` to hide an item from users who lack the policy.

{{ else }}
## Pages and the layout

- The layout `_ModulusLayout` is set in `{{ if ui_engine == "mvc" }}Views/_ViewStart.cshtml{{ else }}Pages/_ViewStart.cshtml{{ end }}`. Every page gets the header, sidebar, theme settings and footer.
- Use the `app-*` tag helpers. The import is `@addTagHelper *, Modulus.AspNetCore.Mvc` in `{{ if ui_engine == "mvc" }}Views/_ViewImports.cshtml{{ else }}Pages/_ViewImports.cshtml{{ end }}`.
- Start a page with the title block: `<app-page-header pretitle="..." title="..." subtitle="..."> ... </app-page-header>`.
- A card: `<app-card title="...">...</app-card>`. Buttons: `<app-button type="primary" icon="plus">...</app-button>`.
  `type="primary"` is the main action; leave `type` out for a secondary one. `html-type="submit"` makes it submit a form. `danger` and `loading` are attributes.

## Forms

- Bind controls with `asp-for`: `<app-form-item asp-for="Name" label="Contact name"><app-input asp-for="Name" /></app-form-item>`.
  The label and the validation message come from the model's data annotations.
- Other controls: `app-input-number`, `app-select`, `app-date-picker`, `app-switch`, `app-checkbox`, `app-textarea`.
- Attributes that are not Modulus attributes (`placeholder`, `hx-*`, `x-*`) go to the real element.
- Forms post normally. Keep the server-side checks: the browser checks are a convenience, not the rule.

## Tables

- A plain table: `<app-table>` with columns as `<thead>` and rows in `<tbody>` (see `app-table` options: `hidden-columns`, `density`, `scroll-x`).
- A server-paged table: the table loads its rows with htmx. The container is a `div` with `hx-get` and
  `hx-trigger="load, customers:refresh from:body"`, so it reloads when a save fires that event.
- Pivot grid, editable grid and nested grid: `app-pivot-grid`, `app-editable-grid`, `app-nested-grid`. Read their rows with `EditableGridForm.Read` or the matching helper.

## Drawers, htmx and saves

The drawer is a side panel with one shared body, `#modulus-drawer-body`. The Alpine store controls it.

```html
<app-button type="primary" icon="plus"
            x-on:click="$store.drawer.load('/Customers/Form', 'New customer')">New customer</app-button>
```

The form inside the drawer posts with htmx. On success the controller answers:

```csharp
Response.HxMessage(MessageType.Success, $"Created {customer.Name}");  // toast
Response.HxCloseDrawer();                                             // closes the drawer
Response.HxTrigger("customers:refresh");                              // refreshes listeners
return NoContent();
```

`MessageType` is `Success`, `Info`, `Warning` or `Error`. Other helpers: `Response.HxNotify`, `HxCloseModal`,
`HxRedirect`. Use `hx-confirm="Delete this customer?"` on a button to ask first.

The button that opens the drawer uses the store: `x-on:click="$store.drawer.load('/Customers/Form', 'New customer')"` (or `$store.drawer.show(title)` when the body is already loaded).

Alpine: `x-data="{ open: false }"`, `x-show`, `x-text`, and `@click` (write `@@click` in a `.cshtml` file, which
Razor reads as `@click`). Alpine and htmx are loaded by the layout, so any page can use them.

The layout adds the antiforgery token to every htmx request, and writes need `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` or a
`POST` with the token. Do not turn it off.

{{ end }}
## Menu, theme and shell content

- The menu is built from every `IMenuContributor`. Its items are `MenuItemDefinition(key, label, href, icon)`, with `Order`
  and `RequiredPolicy`. Child items come from `Children` or `AddChild`.
- The theme lives in `Modulus:Theme` in `appsettings.json`: `Title`, `PrimaryColor`, `DefaultMode` (`Light`, `Dark` or `System`),
  `Layout` (`Horizontal`, `Vertical` or `Condensed`), `Direction` (`Ltr` or `Rtl`), `Cultures` (a language picker when there are two or more).
- Users change the layout in the settings panel. Their choices are kept in the `modulus-theme` cookie.
- The header's user, notifications, apps and tenants come from an `IShellContentProvider`.

## Known limits

- Interactive widgets in MVC and Razor Pages (tree, cascader, transfer, calendar, carousel and similar) need Alpine.
- Date pickers in MVC use the browser's own calendar.
- Icons are Ant Design icons. Use the icon name, for example `plus`, `team`, `edit`.
- The samples' users and data are in memory. Replace them with your own store before production.

## Commands that scaffold UI

`modulus ui add-component <names>`, `modulus ui create-form-from-entity <Entity>`, `modulus ui create-crud <Entity> --module <Module>`,
`modulus ui create-dashboard overview|analytics|reports|audit`, `modulus ui add-chart --type line|column|donut|heatmap`,
`modulus ui add-admin`, `modulus ui add-i18n --languages en,es`, `modulus ui theme set <name>`. Run any command with `--dry-run` first.
